extends ./layout.pug

block title
  | Documentation · Alarm & Alert Service

mixin endpoint(id, method, path, title)
  article.endpoint(id=id)
    .endpoint-head
      span.method(class=`method-${method.toLowerCase()}`)= method
      code.endpoint-path= path
    h4= title
    block

block content
  .docs
    nav.docs-toc(aria-label='On this page')
      p.toc-title Documentation
      ul
        li: a(href='#overview') Overview
        li: a(href='#alerts') Subscriptions & alerts
        li
          a(href='#publishing') Publishing alarms
          ul
            li: a(href='#registering') Registering an alarm
            li: a(href='#creating') Creating alarms
            li: a(href='#periodic') Periodic execution
            li: a(href='#heartbeats') Heartbeats
        li
          a(href='#api') REST API
          ul
            li: a(href='#api-alarm') Alarms
            li: a(href='#api-user') Users
            li: a(href='#api-heartbeat') Heartbeats

    article.docs-body
      section#overview
        h1 Documentation
        p.docs-lead
          | ATLAS Alarm & Alert Service provides a simple way to store generated alarms, handles user subscriptions and generates alerts.

        figure.docs-figure
          img(src='/images/AlarmAndAlert.png' alt='Alarm generators post alarms to the REST API, which stores them in Elasticsearch. Users subscribe through the web frontend and receive alerts.')

        h3 Components
        dl.components
          div
            dt Database
            dd Elasticsearch
          div
            dt REST API & web frontend
            dd Node.js, Express, Pug
          div
            dt Deployment
            dd Docker, Kubernetes
          div
            dt Authentication
            dd Globus (InCommon)
          div
            dt Authorization
            dd API key
          div
            dt Mail
            dd Mailgun

      section#alerts
        h2 Subscriptions & alerts
        p
          | On the #[a(href='/subscriptions') Subscriptions] page, tick the events you want to hear about.
          | Each subscription can be narrowed down with #[strong tags], usually site or network-site names.
          | You are alerted only about alarms that carry at least one of your tags; leave the tags empty to receive all of them.
        p
          | Alerts are bundled into a single email, sent every few hours according to the mail interval in your
          | #[a(href='/profile') preferences]. Turn on vacation mode there to pause them.

      section#publishing
        h2 Publishing alarms
        p
          | A new alarm can be created in three simple steps: register it using the REST API,
          | add the alarm-generating code to your program, and run your program periodically.

        h3#registering Registering an alarm
        p
          | Before registering a new kind of alarm, look at the classification tree of the
          | #[a(href='/alarm/categories') current alarms] and decide whether there already is a category and subcategory where the new alarm would naturally fit.
          | Then describe it in a small JSON document:
        pre: code.
          {
            "category": "Analytics",
            "subcategory": "Frontier",
            "event": "Failed queries",
            "description": "Code running every 1h at UC k8s cluster. Checks all servers for: Rejected queries - server is busy and doesn't respond to the query; DB disconnections - the query was processed by the Frontier server but the Oracle DB terminated the connection. The code can be found here: https://github.com/ATLAS-Analytics/AlarmAndAlertService/blob/master/frontier-failed-q.py",
            "template": "Servers with failed queries:\n%{servers}\n. Affected tasks: \n%{tasks}\n\tConsult the following link to get a table with the most relevant taskids https://atlas-kibana.mwt2.org:5601/s/frontier/goto/c72d263c3e2b86f394ab99211c99b613\n"
          }
        p
          | Write the #[code description] in as much detail as possible: it tells people receiving the alarm how it was generated.
        p
          | The #[code template] defines the text that appears in the alert email. Keep it brief, but give a reader enough to judge how serious the issue is,
          | whether to forward it, and either act on it or start digging further.
          | Every #[code %{variable}] is replaced with the value of #[em variable] from the alarm’s #[code source] field.
          | Only #[code \n] and #[code \t] are available for formatting.
        p
          | Once the document is ready, post it to the #[a(href='#api-alarm-register') register endpoint].

        h3#creating Creating alarms
        p Once the alarm is registered, you can start creating alarms. They are simple JSON documents too:
        pre: code.
          {
            "category": "network",
            "subcategory": "perfsonar",
            "event": "high packet loss",
            "tags": ["UKI-SOUTHGRID-BRIS-HEP"],
            "body": "ps_1 needs 10000ms",
            "source": {
              "src": "137.222.79.1",
              "avg_value": 1.0,
              "host_src": "dice-io-37-00.acrc.bris.ac.uk",
              "site_src": "UKI-SOUTHGRID-BRIS-HEP"
            }
          }
        p In addition to the classification, an alarm contains:
        ul
          li #[code tags]: a single keyword or a short array that users can subscribe to. It lets them limit the number of alerts they receive. Tags must not contain spaces.
          li #[code body]: a very brief description of the event.
          li #[code source]: an optional map whose attributes are placed into the alert template.
        p Send the document to the #[a(href='#api-alarm-create') create endpoint].

        h3#periodic Periodic execution
        p
          | Alarm-generating code is typically run on a schedule, for example as a cron job, so that new conditions are detected and posted as they happen.

        h3#heartbeats Heartbeats
        p
          | Heartbeats catch services that silently stop working. Register a heartbeat with an #[code interval] (seconds) and #[code min_expected] count,
          | then have your service post heartbeats regularly. If fewer than #[code min_expected] heartbeats arrive within the interval, an alarm is raised.
        pre: code.
          {
            "category": "SLATE",
            "subcategory": "Squid",
            "event": "server down",
            "template": "Squid instance: %{instance}, running at %{site}, failed to send required number of heartbeats.",
            "description": "This alarm gets generated if a squid instance does not send at least 4 heartbeats in last 60 seconds. Heartbeats are sent in 10 second intervals.",
            "interval": 60,
            "min_expected": 4
          }

      section#api
        h2 REST API
        p The API has three parts: alarms, users and heartbeats. All request and response bodies are JSON.

        h3#api-alarm Alarms

        +endpoint('api-alarm-categories', 'GET', '/alarm/categories', 'List categories')
          p Returns all currently registered alarm categories.

        +endpoint('api-alarm-tags', 'GET', '/alarm/tags', 'List tags')
          p Returns every tag and site name found in stored alarms. Used for suggestions when subscribing.

        +endpoint('api-alarm-register', 'POST', '/alarm/category', 'Register an alarm')
          pre: code.
            {
              "category": "",
              "subcategory": "",
              "event": "",
              "description": "",
              "template": ""
            }

        +endpoint('api-alarm-edit', 'PATCH', '/alarm/category', 'Edit an alarm')
          pre: code.
            {
              "category": "",
              "subcategory": "",
              "event": "",
              "description": "",
              "template": ""
            }

        +endpoint('api-alarm-create', 'POST', '/alarm/', 'Create an alarm')
          pre: code.
            {
              "category": "",
              "subcategory": "",
              "event": "",
              "tags": ["", ""],
              "body": "",
              "source": { "xxx": "", "yyy": "", ... }
            }

        +endpoint('api-alarm-list', 'POST', '/alarm/fetch', 'List alarms')
          p #[code period] is a number of hours.
          pre: code.
            {
              "category": "",
              "subcategory": "",
              "event": "",
              "period": 6
            }

        +endpoint('api-alarm-delete', 'DELETE', '/alarm/', 'Delete an alarm category')
          pre: code.
            {
              "category": "",
              "subcategory": "",
              "event": ""
            }
          p.warning Unregisters the alarm event. Use with caution!

        h3#api-user Users

        +endpoint('api-user-list', 'GET', '/user/', 'List users')
          p Returns information on all users.

        +endpoint('api-user-info', 'GET', '/user/:userId', 'User information')
          p Returns information on a specific user, including preferences and subscriptions.

        +endpoint('api-user-delete', 'DELETE', '/user/:userId', 'Full unsubscribe')
          p Completely deletes a user from the system.

        +endpoint('api-user-preferences', 'POST', '/user/preferences/:userId', 'Update preferences')
          pre: code.
            {
              "vacation": true
            }
          p Only preferences supported by the deployed system are allowed.

        +endpoint('api-user-subscriptions', 'POST', '/user/subscriptions/:userId', 'Update subscriptions')
          pre: code.
            [
              {
                "category": "",
                "subcategory": "",
                "event": "",
                "tags": "MWT2 AGLT2"
              },
              ...
            ]
          p
            | The body is the complete list of the user’s subscriptions; an empty array unsubscribes from everything.
            | #[code tags] is a space-separated string, or #[code "*"] for all.

        h3#api-heartbeat Heartbeats

        +endpoint('api-heartbeat-categories', 'GET', '/heartbeat/', 'List heartbeats')
          p Returns all registered heartbeats.

        +endpoint('api-heartbeat-register', 'POST', '/heartbeat/register', 'Register a heartbeat')
          pre: code.
            {
              "category": "",
              "subcategory": "",
              "event": "",
              "description": "",
              "template": "",
              "interval": 60,
              "min_expected": 4
            }

        +endpoint('api-heartbeat-edit', 'PATCH', '/heartbeat/', 'Edit a heartbeat')
          pre: code.
            {
              "category": "",
              "subcategory": "",
              "event": "",
              "description": "",
              "template": "",
              "interval": 60,
              "min_expected": 4
            }

        +endpoint('api-heartbeat-create', 'POST', '/heartbeat/', 'Send a heartbeat')
          pre: code.
            {
              "category": "",
              "subcategory": "",
              "event": "",
              "source": { "site": "MWT2", "instance": "MWT2_Slate_01" }
            }

        +endpoint('api-heartbeat-list', 'POST', '/heartbeat/fetch', 'List received heartbeats')
          p #[code period] is a number of hours.
          pre: code.
            {
              "category": "",
              "subcategory": "",
              "event": "",
              "period": 6
            }

        +endpoint('api-heartbeat-delete', 'DELETE', '/heartbeat/', 'Delete a heartbeat')
          pre: code.
            {
              "category": "",
              "subcategory": "",
              "event": ""
            }
          p.warning Unregisters the heartbeat. Use with caution!

block append scripts
  script(src='/js/docs.js' defer)
